digicarlo (1.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * The window is a garage, pre-rendered in POV-Ray the way a 1995 CD-ROM
    game was, in a Windows 95 frame. The card in the reader and the camera
    on the bench pull; the darkroom door and START develop; the calendar
    shows today and the card's tag how many pictures are new.
  * The Photo Board: every waiting shot pinned up as a print, sessions led
    by index cards. The date stamp, the alarm clock, the eraser and the
    wastebasket on its ledge set dates, keep the camera's, go back to
    automatic and leave shots out.
  * ROTATE, NAME and PLACE on the board's radio: a quarter turn (the EXIF
    orientation for a JPEG, so nothing is recompressed), a title, and GPS
    coordinates from saved places, all written into the library copy.
    UNDO takes back one change at a time.
  * The red-eye pen: the fixes the face finder suggests, checked photo by
    photo, dropped with a click, or added by clicking a missed eye.
  * What is decided on the board is kept in board.json until developed.
  * Pulls from a camera whose clock can be right (trust_clock_cameras: the
    Polaroid i1237 and Konica KD-400Z) ask which sessions to date by it.
  * The garage's radio: CHECK CARD reads a card through and looks for read
    errors in the kernel's log; EJECT unmounts it and powers off the
    reader; ERASE CARD empties it, only when every file on it is in the
    archive byte for byte; SYNC asks Syncthing how the library is getting
    to the phone. Developing asks Syncthing to rescan the library at once.
  * digicarlo-gui --classic opens the window of 1.1 and 1.2.
  * fonts-glasstty and fonts-comic-neue are recommended, for the green
    screen and the handwriting.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Wed, 23 Sep 2026 22:00:00 +0000

digicarlo (1.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * digicarlo redeye PHOTO... --out DIR takes the flash red out of eyes,
    into copies. Faces are found by YuNet, run in plain numpy; numpy is
    recommended.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Wed, 23 Sep 2026 20:00:00 +0000

digicarlo (1.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * The window is now a cartoon car. The shots sit in a landscape seen
    through the windshield, and the controls live on the dashboard of a
    Nash Metropolitan, the way Putt-Putt's did: the cameras and cards in
    the glove box, a horn that honks and looks for cameras again, a radio
    whose preset keys date the selected shots, a fuel gauge for free space,
    a clock, and a big red START button.
  * The paint is the Metropolitan's actual Caribbean Blue (PPG P-905,
    Ditzler 41161), paired with Snowberry White, instead of a generic
    powder blue.
  * Text is anti-aliased and strokes are at least two pixels wide. The
    unsmoothed 8-point font of 1.0 came out thin and broken on Wayland at
    fractional scales such as 150%.
  * The UI font is Nunito; fonts-nunito is now recommended.
  * Sounds: the horn honks, and a finished job beeps twice. View > Sounds,
    or 'digicarlo config set sounds no', turns them off.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Wed, 23 Sep 2026 17:00:00 +0000

digicarlo (1.0.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * First release.
  * Pulls from memory cards (mounted read-only through udisks when the
    desktop has not mounted them), PTP cameras through gphoto2, the SiPix
    Blink II through Blinky 1.6.1's driver, and any folder.
  * Every file is archived byte for byte and read back before it counts;
    a file a failing card will not give up is reported and the rest carry on.
  * Dates come from when the pictures came off the camera. Sessions are
    found where the camera's clock was reset, the gaps within each are kept,
    and any run of shots can be given its own start or keep the camera's
    date.
  * MOV, AVI and MTS clips are remuxed to MP4 with the video stream copied
    bit for bit, checked by hashing it in both files; 8-bit PCM and other
    audio MP4 cannot hold becomes AAC.
  * digicarlo-gui: Windows 95 dressed as a Nash Metropolitan.

 -- antair <antairdo@gmail.com>  Wed, 23 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
